# `shamefully-hoist=true` forces pnpm to flatten every transitive
# dependency into the top-level node_modules, even when the same package
# exists at multiple versions under different parents. The desktop
# workspace uses pnpm with `node-linker=hoisted` (so `pnpm install`
# populates apps/desktop/node_modules/ as a single tree), but pnpm's
# default hoisting still leaves ~250 packages nested under
# `<pkg>/node_modules/<dep>/` whenever two parents disagree on the
# version. electron-builder's asar packer copies only the *top* level
# package directories into app.asar, so anything nested is invisible at
# runtime and the app crashes with `Cannot find module '<x>'` as soon as
# a transitive require walks back to a nested copy.
#
# This mirrors the long-standing recommendation in
# electron-builder#8117 (and the `shamefully-hoist=true` workaround that
# the Next.js and Vite docs use for monorepo Electron builds). The
# repeated-package cost (multiple on-disk copies of, e.g., `minimatch`
# 3/5/9/10) is acceptable here because Electron apps are run once per
# install, the asar contents are deduped at pack time, and the desktop
# tarball already weighs ~210 MB.
shamefully-hoist=true
node-linker=hoisted
public-hoist-pattern[]=*
